---
import DocShell from '../../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../../lib/nav';

const toc = [
   { id: 'fields', label: 'Violation fields' },
   { id: 'impact', label: 'Impact levels' },
   { id: 'node', label: 'Failing nodes' },
   { id: 'rule', label: 'Finding the fix' },
   { id: 'unmapped', label: 'Rules without a criterion' },
];
---

<DocShell
   title="Fix an axe violation"
   summary="axe-core is the open-source rule engine most automated accessibility checkers use, and a1 axe runs its rules against a page. A violation has a rule, an impact, and the elements that failed the rule. Read the failing element, then map the rule to the success criterion it fails: one testable pass/fail rule inside the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG), the standard most accessibility requirements come from. a1 wcag rule finds the criterion and the techniques that fix it."
   links={docsLinks}
   toc={toc}
>
   <section class="panel" id="fields">
      <h2>Violation fields</h2>
      <p>
         <code>a1 axe</code> injects axe-core into the page and runs its rules there, so it sees
         the rendered DOM instead of the HTML a server sent. It returns four arrays:
         <code>violations</code>, <code>passes</code>, <code>incomplete</code> for checks axe
         could not decide on its own, and <code>inapplicable</code> for rules that do not apply
         to this page.
      </p>
      <p>
         Each violation has the axe rule id, its impact, a description, help text and a help
         URL, the WCAG and other standard tags it maps to, and the <code>nodes</code> that
         failed.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 axe http://localhost:3000/checkout --json</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>"violations": [
  &#123;
    "id": "button-name",
    "impact": "critical",
    "description": "Ensure buttons have discernible text",
    "help": "Buttons must have discernible text",
    "helpUrl": "https://dequeuniversity.com/rules/axe/4.13/button-name?application=axeAPI",
    "tags": ["cat.name-role-value", "wcag2a", "wcag412", "ACT", "..."],
    "nodes": [
      &#123;
        "target": ["button[type=\"button\"]"],
        "html": "&lt;button type=\"button\"&gt;&lt;img src=\"cart.png\" alt=\"\"&gt;&lt;/button&gt;",
        "failureSummary": "Fix any of the following:\n  Element does not have inner text that is visible to screen readers\n  aria-label attribute does not exist or is empty\n  ..."
      &#125;
    ]
  &#125;
]</code></pre>
   </section>

   <section class="ledger" id="impact">
      <h2>Impact levels</h2>
      <p>
         Impact comes from axe-core, one fixed value per rule, not per WCAG criterion. It says
         how badly the rule's kind of failure affects a user, not how many users hit it on this
         page.
      </p>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>critical</code>
            <span>Blocks access outright. <code>button-name</code> and <code>image-alt</code> are
            critical: a control or image with no accessible name is unusable, not just harder to
            use.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>serious</code>
            <span><code>color-contrast</code> and <code>link-name</code> are serious: text is
            there, but a low-vision or screen reader user has real trouble getting to it.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>moderate</code>
            <span><code>heading-order</code> is moderate: a heading level that skips from 1 to 3
            confuses navigation without blocking it.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>minor</code>
            <span><code>empty-heading</code> is minor: an empty heading adds noise to the
            structure without hiding content.</span>
         </div>
      </div>
      <p>
         <code>--fail-on &lt;impact&gt;</code> on <code>axe</code> and <code>audit</code> counts
         only violations at or above that level toward the exit code, so
         <code>--fail-on serious</code> lets a page ship with open moderate and minor findings
         while still failing on critical and serious ones.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="node">
      <h2>Failing nodes</h2>
      <p>
         Each entry in <code>nodes</code> is one element that failed the rule.
         <code>target</code> is a CSS selector array. Paste
         <code>document.querySelector('button[type="button"]')</code> into the browser
         console to find the element. <code>html</code> is the failing markup as axe saw it.
         <code>failureSummary</code> lists every way to satisfy the rule, not just the one
         this element is missing, since axe cannot tell which fix you will pick.
      </p>
      <p>
         A rule can have more than one failing node, and <code>axe</code> reports every one
         it found, not just the first.
      </p>
      <p>
         A page with fewer than 10 elements and under 50 characters of text adds a warning
         that a single-page app may not have mounted. See
         <a href="/guides/troubleshooting#thin">empty results</a>.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="rule">
      <h2>Finding the fix</h2>
      <p>
         <code>a1 wcag rule &lt;ruleId&gt;</code> maps one axe rule to the criteria it fails, each
         with its conformance level (A, AA, or AAA), techniques, and failures, plus the axe
         help text and URL. <code>a1 audit</code> prints the command for you: every failing
         rule gets an entry in <code>result.nextCommands</code>.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 wcag rule button-name --json</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>"ruleId": "button-name",
"criteria": [
  &#123;
    "id": "4.1.2",
    "title": "Name, Role, Value",
    "level": "A",
    "techniques": [
      &#123; "id": "ARIA14", "title": "Using aria-label to provide an accessible name where a visible label cannot be used", "..." : "..." &#125;,
      &#123; "id": "H44", "title": "Using label elements to associate text labels with form controls", "..." : "..." &#125;,
      "..."
    ],
    "failures": [
      &#123; "id": "F68", "title": "Failure of Success Criterion 4.1.2 due to a user interface control not having a programmatically determined name" &#125;,
      "..."
    ]
  &#125;
],
"description": "Ensure buttons have discernible text",
"help": "Buttons must have discernible text",
"helpUrl": "https://dequeuniversity.com/rules/axe/4.13/button-name?application=axeAPI"</code></pre>
      <p>
         For the <code>&lt;button type="button"&gt;&lt;img src="cart.png" alt=""&gt;&lt;/button&gt;</code>
         node above, the fix is <code>ARIA14</code>: add an <code>aria-label</code> to the button,
         or put visible text inside it. <code>H44</code> applies to form controls that take a
         <code>&lt;label&gt;</code>, not to a button.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="field-note" id="unmapped">
      <h2>Rules without a criterion</h2>
      <p>
         <code>a1 wcag rule heading-order</code> returns 0, with an empty
         <code>criteria</code> array. axe tags <code>heading-order</code> as a best practice
         rather than a specific success criterion, so no page in WCAG decides whether to fix it.
         Fix it anyway.
      </p>
      <p>
         A default <code>a1 axe</code> run covers only the rules mapped to a WCAG criterion, so
         axe runs a best-practice rule like this one only when you name it with
         <code>--rule</code>.
      </p>
   </section>
</DocShell>
